## Chemical reduction with $\mathbf{CoCp}_{2}^{*}$ with and without $\mathbf{Na}^{+}$
To probe whether $\mathrm{e^{-}/N a^{+}}$ reduction occurred only at the surface or throughout the bulk of MIL-125, we examined chemical reductions using batches of the MOF that differed in their size and morphology. The smallest crystals (batch 2) were roughly spherical with a diameter of $85\pm16.5\ \mathrm{nm}$ , and the largest (batch 3) were octahedral, with axes of $3650\pm1350\mathrm{nm}$ . If reduction were limited to the surface $\mathrm{Ti}_{8}$ clusters, then these two batches should have given different extents of reduction of MIL-125, measured as the number of $\mathrm{e}^{-}$ per $\mathrm{Ti}_{8}$ node in the entire sample. However, the extent of reduction does not depend on the crystal size (Fig. 5 and 6). This shows that reduction is not dependent on external surface area, that it is occurring at all of the nodes throughout the MOF. Thus, the $\mathrm{e}^{-}/$ $\mathbf{Na}^{+}$ can migrate into the MOF pores. This conclusion is consistent with the very high level of reduction observed, $1{-}8\mathrm{e}^{-}$ per $\mathrm{Ti}_{8}$ node throughout the bulk of the material. There are simply not enough $\mathrm{Ti}_{8}$ clusters at the crystal surface to accept that many electrons, given the small ratio of surface to bulk for these sized crystals.
## Soluble cluster analogues
To better understand the redox chemistry at a molecular level, soluble cluster analogues were prepared and studied.
$\mathrm{Ti_{8}O_{8}}(\mathrm{OOC}^{t}\mathrm{Bu})_{16}\left(\cdot\right.$ 1) was prepared as described36 and isolated as a white crystalline solid. Cluster 1 is comprised of eight roughly octahedral Ti(IV) centers arranged in a cyclic array, with each Ti linked to its neighbors by one oxo and two pivalate $(^{t}\mathbf{BuCOO}^{-})$ bridging ligands. The solution ${}^{1}\mathrm{H}$ NMR spectrum ( $\mathrm{{\dot{d}_{8}}}$ -toluene) of 1 features two sharp resonances at 1.34 and 1.22 ppm corresponding to $^{t}\mathbf{B}\mathbf{u}$ groups that are axial and equatorial with respect to the planar $\mathrm{Ti}_{8}$ ring. The ligand arrangement differs from that of the nodes in MIL-125 in that the latter has four of the carboxylates replaced by bridging hydroxo groups to give an empirical cluster formula of $\mathrm{Ti_{8}O_{8}(O H)_{4}(O O C R)_{12}}$ . Also, 1 features alkyl carboxylates rather than aryl carboxylates as found in MIL-125. While the molecular cluster with benzoate ligands can be prepared,36 the limited solubility and uninformative $^1\mathrm{H}$ NMR spectrum of $\mathrm{{Ti}_{8}\mathrm{{O}_{8}(\mathrm{{OOCPh})_{16}}}}$ makes it unsuitable for these studies.
The X-ray crystal structure of 2-Na shows that the $\mathrm{Ti}_{8}$ ring is intact, albeit distorted, with a $\mathrm{{Na}^{+}}$ cation coordinated on one edge of the inside of the ring. The $\mathbf{Na}^{+}$ coordinates two THF molecules and the two $\scriptstyle\left|\mu-\mathbf{oxo}\right.$ ligands that ank Ti6. The Ti6–O distances of 1.9556(26) and 1.9473(27) $\mathring\mathbf{A}$ are ${\sim}0.12\mathring\mathrm{A}$ elongated from the mean Ti–O distance in 1 $(\mathrm{Ti}{\bf-O}_{\mathrm{ave}}=1.827\mathrm{~\AA};$ see Fig. 6).27 Moving around the ring from Ti6 the Ti–O distances alternate long and short. Next to Ti6 this difference is quite pronounced, with the two Ti–O distances for Ti5 and Ti7 differing by $\sim0.15\mathrm{~\AA~}$ . At Ti2 on the other side of the ring, however, the two Ti–O distances are almost the same, 1.7939(24) vs. 1.8090(25) $\mathring{\mathbf{A}}.$ , and are even shorter than the average in the fully oxidized 1. These metrical parameters indicate that the added electron is mostly localized on Ti6 in the solid-state, near the $\mathbf{Na}^{+}$ , but that the inuence of the added electron is clearly felt in the neighbouring octahedra. The addition of the $\mathbf{Na}^{+}$ renders the ring more elliptical, with distances between titanium atoms that are across the ring varying by $0.584\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ in 2-Na (vs. only $0.268\mathring{\mathbf{A}}$ in 1).
