## Discussion
## Reduction occurs throughout the MOF, not just at the surface
MIL-125 and ${\bf N H}_{2}–\bf M I L-125$ have previously been shown to be redox-active, like other titanium-oxo containing MOFs.23,30,37,38 UV irradiation (or visible for the amino derivative) in the presence of an alcohol causes reduction of the MOF, as indicated by the blue colour of the reduced material. EPR spectroscopy suggests that the reduction is Ti-based.5,23,30 We have recently shown that this reduction corresponds to a maximum of ${\sim}2\mathrm{e}^{-}$ per $\mathrm{Ti_{8}O_{8}}$ node. That reduction is a proton-coupled electron transfer (PCET) process, so each $\mathrm{Ti}_{8}$ cluster is reduced by $({\mathrm e}^{-}+$ $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{H}}^{+}$ ), a net H-atom.31 Similar chemistry was shown for the COK-69 MOF that contains a $\mathrm{[Ti_{3}^{\mathrm{~IV}}(\upmu^{3}{-}O)(O)_{2}(R C O O)_{6}]}$ cluster at each node.5
The studies reported here provide a detailed look at the cation-coupled reduction of MIL-125 and ${\bf N H}_{2}–\mathbf{MIL}–125$ . Using organometallic, soluble reductants and $\mathrm{{Na}^{+}}$ salts, and with the preparation of molecular versions of the reduced $\mathrm{Ti}_{8}\mathrm{O}_{8}$ nodes, this work provides an unusually detailed and quantitative view of the reduction of a MOF.
These very high charging levels are consistent only with reduction throughout the MIL-125 crystallites. There are simply not enough titanium ions in the surface layer of the MOF to accommodate all of these electrons (in the limit of $8\mathrm{e}^{-}$ per node, formally every titanium has been reduced to $\mathrm{Ti}\big(\mathrm{III}\big)\dot{}$ ). In addition, different batches of MIL-125 with different sizes and morphologies behave very similarly, showing that reduction is not simply a surface property. This reduction throughout the material, as discussed in the next section, is possible because the $\mathbf{Na}^{+}$ ions migrate into the pores to provide local charge balance for the added electrons.
## Roughly $\mathbf{1}:\mathbf{1}\mathbf{Na}^{+}$ to $\mathbf{e}^{-}$ stoichiometry of MIL-125 reduction
The cryptand does not remove all of the $\mathrm{{Na}^{+}}$ likely due this being an equilibrium process. Some insight into the binding of $\mathbf{Na}^{+}$ in MIL-125 is provided by the synthesis and characterization of molecular analogues of the $\mathrm{Ti}_{8}\mathrm{O}_{8}$ MOF nodes. The crystal structure of 2-Na shows the sodium ion chelated by two oxide ligands to a single titanium ion, which is likely to have the highest $\mathrm{Ti}(\mathrm{m})$ character. However, NMR spectra of the molecular clusters in solution show that the sodium is rapidly moving around the $\mathrm{Ti_{8}O_{8}}$ ring such that the high symmetry of the oxidized form is retained. This molecular material likely gives a detailed sense of what is likely occurring inside $\mathrm{\bfMIL}{\bf-}125+\bigl(\mathrm{Na}^{+}+\mathrm{e}^{-}\bigr).$ , including the low barrier to $\mathbf{Na}^{+}$ migration around the $\mathrm{Ti_{8}O_{8}}$ ring. A similar mode of cation coordination has been suggested to occur in the electrochemical lithiation of UiO-66 $\left(\mathrm{Zr_{6}O_{4}(O H)_{4}(b d c)_{6}}\right)$ .39
